.Dd August 12, 2026
.Dt SKYWALKCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm skywalkctl
.Nd inspect and debug the macOS Skywalk networking subsystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar arguments
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an Apple diagnostic utility for the Skywalk networking subsystem.
Skywalk supplies plumbing between networking software, kernel datapaths,
and supported hardware.
The utility exposes providers, nexus instances, channels, flows,
interfaces, allocator state, namespace reservations, and protocol counters.
.Pp
This manual expands the brief manual and built-in usage text shipped with
the tested Apple-signed binary.
It is based on macOS 26.4 build 25E246.
The installed binary and its own help remain authoritative because
.Nm
is an unstable debugging interface, not a documented public API.
.Pp
Most commands are observational, but
.Cm enable ,
some forms of
.Cm log ,
.Cm traffic-rule ,
and every functional form of
.Cm redirect
change live or persistent state.
Those commands are clearly marked below.
.Sh PRIVILEGES
Some output is available to an ordinary user, but complete kernel statistics
commonly require root.
An empty result accompanied by
.Dq Operation not permitted
is a privilege coverage gap, not proof that the requested object does not exist.
.Sh OBJECT MODEL
.Bl -tag -width flow-switch
.It Sy provider
A definition for a Skywalk service, such as a network interface,
flow switch, user pipe, or kernel pipe.
.It Sy nexus
A runtime instance created from a provider.
.It Sy channel
An endpoint attached to a nexus port.
Output may associate a channel with a process and file descriptor.
.It Sy flow
A Skywalk network-flow record.
A row can outlive the corresponding application socket and is not necessarily
a currently established connection.
.It Sy flow-switch
The Skywalk component that classifies and moves packets for an interface.
.It Sy netif
Skywalk's representation of a network interface.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss channel
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl channel [-h] [-C command] [-w seconds]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays channel instances, nexus UUIDs, port numbers, process/PID attribution,
file descriptors, and channel flags.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage.
.It Fl C , Fl -command Ns = Ns Ar command
Show channels attributed to the named command.
.It Fl w , Fl -wait Ns = Ns Ar seconds
Repeat at the requested interval until interrupted.
This is an unbounded mode; callers should impose their own timeout.
.El
.Pp
In the tested snapshot, the unfiltered command reported 31 instances.
Filtering for
.Pa kernel_task
returned kernel-owned ports.
The output flag
.Dv DEFUNCT_OK
is a channel capability/state flag and is not evidence of malicious activity.
.Ss flow
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flow [-h] [-C command] [-I interface] [-n] [-J]
                [-p protocol] [-P pid] [-w seconds]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays flow records and accounting.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C , Fl -command Ns = Ns Ar command
Filter by effective command name.
.It Fl I , Fl -interface Ns = Ns Ar interface
Filter by interface name.
.It Fl n , Fl -numeric
Do not resolve addresses or service names.
.It Fl J , Fl -json
Emit JSON.
.It Fl p , Fl -protocol Ns = Ns Ar protocol
Filter by protocol, such as
.Cm tcp .
.It Fl P , Fl -pid Ns = Ns Ar pid
Filter by PID.
.It Fl w , Fl -wait Ns = Ns Ar seconds
Repeat until interrupted.
.El
.Pp
The table contains protocol, local and remote endpoint, inbound and outbound
byte counters, packet counters, service class, interface, nexus port,
flow-advisory index, compact flags, tracking states, RTT values, and process
attribution.
The secondary packet counters labeled
.Dq SPkts
are not defined by the installed manual and should be retained without guessing
at their precise implementation meaning.
.Pp
The flag legend printed by the tested binary is:
.Bl -tag -compact -width Ds
.It Cm t
tracked
.It Cm c
connected
.It Cm l
listener
.It Cm q
QoS marking
.It Cm w
wait-close
.It Cm e
close notification
.It Cm A
aborted
.It Cm N
nonviable
.It Cm W
withdrawn
.It Cm T
torn down
.It Cm D
destroyed
.It Cm R
lingering
.It Cm L
low latency
.It Cm P
parent
.It Cm C
child
.It Cm S
do not wake from sleep
.El
.Pp
On the tested build,
.Fl J
produced syntactically valid JSON but repeated the
.Dq localTrackState
key twice per object and omitted
.Dq remoteTrackState .
Consumers must account for duplicate-key ambiguity.
.Ss flow-adv
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flow-adv [-h] [-C command] [-I interface] [-P pid]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays flow-advisory records.
The three filters select command, interface, and PID respectively.
All tested forms completed successfully with no records.
That means no advisory record was returned at those instants; it is not a
historical guarantee.
.Ss flow-owner
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flow-owner [-h] [-C command] [-I interface] [-P pid]
.Ed
.Pp
Maps flow-owner properties to interface, nexus port, property, bucket, and
process.
In the tested snapshot, the unfiltered view contained kernel ownership rows for
the primary Wi-Fi and one tunnel interface.
.Ss flow-route
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flow-route [-h] [-n]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays Skywalk flow-route entries.
.Fl n
disables name and service resolution.
Both tested forms completed successfully with no entries.
.Ss flow-switch
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flow-switch [-h] [-G] [-I interface] [-U uuid] [-v]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays flow-switch counters.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl G , Fl -global
Fold counters across instances.
.It Fl I , Fl -interface Ns = Ns Ar interface
Select one interface.
.It Fl U , Fl -uuid Ns = Ns Ar uuid
Select one nexus UUID.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Include detailed counters.
.El
.Pp
Counters cover receive/transmit processing, classification, aggregation,
copies, queue/ring conditions, fragmentation, allocation failures, and drops.
They are generally cumulative for the lifetime of the object or boot.
A counter whose label contains
.Dq dropped
does not by itself establish user-visible packet loss or compromise.
Capture two snapshots over a known interval and interpret the reason-specific
counter with neighboring counters and traffic volume.
.Ss interface
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl interface [-h] [-G] [-I interface] [-L] [-Q] [-W seconds]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays Skywalk netif counters.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl G , Fl -global
Fold statistics globally.
.It Fl I , Fl -interface Ns = Ns Ar interface
Select an interface.
.It Fl L , Fl -llink
Display logical-link and queue-set topology.
.It Fl Q , Fl -queue
Display netif queue rates, totals, service classes, and queue parameters.
.It Fl W , Fl -wait Ns = Ns Ar seconds
Repeat until interrupted.
.El
.Pp
Queue service-class abbreviations include BE (best effort), BK (background),
VI (video), and VO (voice).
Zero instantaneous queue rates only describe the sampling instant.
.Ss memory
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl memory [-h] [-I interface] [-J] [-P pid]
                  [-a] [-A] [-R] [-C] [-g]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays Skywalk allocator information.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl I , Fl -interface Ns = Ns Ar interface
Filter by interface.
.It Fl J , Fl -json
Emit JSON.
.It Fl P , Fl -pid Ns = Ns Ar pid
Filter by PID.
.It Fl a , Fl -all
Show arenas, regions, and caches.
.It Fl A , Fl -arena
Show arenas.
.It Fl R , Fl -region
Show regions.
.It Fl C , Fl -cache
Show caches.
.It Fl g , Fl -group
Group memory by process.
.El
.Pp
Arenas associate clients with region types; regions describe segment/object
geometry and memory totals; caches describe slabs, magazines, allocations,
frees, failures, utilization, and backing regions.
These are kernel allocator statistics, not application heap measurements.
A full cache or nonzero cumulative failure counter is not automatically a leak.
.Ss netns
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl netns -a
skywalkctl netns -i ip-address -p protocol
.Ed
.Pp
Displays TCP or UDP port reservations by network namespace.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a , Fl -all
Show all namespaces.
This is a complete primary mode.
.It Fl i , Fl -ip Ns = Ns Ar address
Select an IPv4 or IPv6 namespace address.
.It Fl p , Fl -protocol Ns = Ns Ar protocol
Select
.Cm tcp
or
.Cm udp .
.El
.Pp
Unless
.Fl a
is present, both
.Fl i
and
.Fl p
are required.
When
.Fl a
is present, additional namespace specifiers are ignored and the tool prints a
warning.
A valid address/protocol pair with no matching namespace may exit 2 silently.
Malformed or incomplete input exits 22 with an explanatory message.
.Pp
Columns report the port, Skywalk reservation count, BSD reservation count,
and listener count.
A reservation is not necessarily a listening user process; correlate with
.Xr lsof 8
or system
.Xr netstat 1 .
.Ss protons
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl protons
.Ed
.Pp
Displays protocol-number reference counts and PID/effective-PID fields.
The numeric protocol field corresponds to the IP protocol/IPv6 next-header
number space; consult the IANA protocol-number registry for names.
.Pp
Although the tested binary advertised
.Fl a ,
.Fl i ,
and
.Fl p ,
it rejected all three as invalid options.
Only the no-option form was usable on the tested build.
.Ss netstat
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl netstat -a [-C command | -I interface | -P pid | -U uuid]
                    [-p protocol] [-n] [-v]
skywalkctl netstat -s [-C command | -I interface | -P pid | -U uuid]
                    [-p protocol] [-G] [-o] [-v] [-z] [-n]
skywalkctl netstat -a -s [modifiers]
.Ed
.Pp
This is the Skywalk subcommand, not the separate system
.Xr netstat 1
program.
It requires a primary mode:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a , Fl -all
Select the flow view.
.It Fl s , Fl -statistics
Select per-protocol statistics.
.El
.Pp
Filters and modifiers are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl C , Fl -command Ns = Ns Ar command
Filter by command.
.It Fl G , Fl -global
Fold statistics globally.
.It Fl I , Fl -interface Ns = Ns Ar interface
Filter by interface.
.It Fl n , Fl -numeric
Disable address and service-name resolution.
.It Fl o , Fl -aop
Include AOP statistics in statistics mode.
.It Fl P , Fl -pid Ns = Ns Ar pid
Filter by PID.
.It Fl p , Fl -protocol Ns = Ns Ar protocol
Filter by protocol.
.It Fl U , Fl -uuid Ns = Ns Ar uuid
Filter by nexus UUID.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Expand output.
.It Fl z , Fl -zero
Include zero-valued statistics.
.El
.Pp
Filters or modifiers without
.Fl a
or
.Fl s
exit 64 and print usage.
.Fl a Fl s
produces both flow and statistics views.
In statistics mode,
.Fl G
cannot be combined with interface, PID, command, or UUID filters.
On the tested build, nonnumeric
.Fl a
could spend substantial time resolving names; use
.Fl a Fl n
for reproducible evidence.
.Ss provider
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl provider [-h] [-D]
.Ed
.Pp
Lists nexus providers and child instances.
.Fl D
adds transmit/receive ring counts, slots, buffer and metadata sizes, memory-hint
counts, and instance UUIDs.
Provider types observed on the tested system were net-if, flow-switch,
user-pipe, and kernel-pipe.
.Ss show
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl show [-h] [-v]
.Ed
.Pp
Prints a concise runtime overview.
.Fl v
adds attached process/port information for applicable channels.
It is a useful first inventory, but
.Cm tree
and
.Cm provider
provide more structure.
.Ss tree
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl tree [-h] [-U uuid]
.Ed
.Pp
Emits the provider, nexus, and channel hierarchy as JSON.
.Fl U
starts at the specified object UUID.
Validate saved output with
.Dl jq -e . tree.json
before parsing it.
.Ss log
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl log show
skywalkctl log list
skywalkctl log SK_VERB_name
skywalkctl log 0
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm show
Read-only.
Show currently selected verbose flags.
.It Cm list
Read-only.
List every known verbose flag and numeric bit value.
.It Ar SK_VERB_name
.Em State-changing .
Set the kernel Skywalk verbosity mask represented by the flag expression.
.It Cm 0
.Em State-changing .
Reset the kernel Skywalk verbosity mask.
.El
.Pp
Changing verbosity can substantially increase kernel logging.
The state-changing forms were not executed during this research.
.Ss status
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl status
.Ed
.Pp
Checks a legacy Skywalk enable/boot setting.
On the tested build it queried a sysctl that did not exist, printed
.Dq Skywalk is NOT enabled currently,
and exited 71 even though every runtime inventory command returned populated
Skywalk objects.
Do not use this command alone as a runtime-presence test.
.Ss enable
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl enable 1
skywalkctl enable 0
.Ed
.Pp
.Em State-changing and root-only .
Edits NVRAM boot arguments to enable (1) or disable (0) Skywalk and may require
a reboot.
The installed binary says the change may take effect after reboot.
Neither form was executed during this research.
.Ss tcpinfo
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl tcpinfo local-ip local-port remote-ip remote-port
.Ed
.Pp
Queries one exact TCP four-tuple.
Output fields are interface index, sequence number, acknowledgement number,
receive window, and window scale.
The tuple must still be present when queried; otherwise the command reports
.Dq flow not found .
.Ss flowidns
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl flowidns [-v] [-d domain] [-f hex-flow-id]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays flow-ID namespace allocation statistics and mappings.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
Show records as well as aggregate statistics.
.It Fl d , Fl -domain Ns = Ns Ar domain
Select PF, IPSec, flowswitch, or inpcb.
.It Fl f , Fl -flowID Ns = Ns Ar hex-id
Select one hexadecimal flow ID.
.El
.Pp
Aggregate fields are allocations, releases, collisions, and current flow-ID
count.
Record fields include flow ID, endpoint pair, IP protocol number, ports, and
domain.
A flow ID is an internal identifier, not an external identity.
.Ss traffic-rule
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl traffic-rule show
skywalkctl traffic-rule add -t inet -p protocol [-l local-address]
        [-r remote-address] [-L local-port] [-R remote-port]
        -q queue-set [-i interface]
skywalkctl traffic-rule add -t eth [-e ether-type] [-m remote-mac]
        -q queue-set [-i interface]
skywalkctl traffic-rule remove -u rule-uuid
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm show
Read-only.
List installed traffic-steering rules.
.It Cm add
.Em State-changing .
Add an inet or Ethernet steering rule.
Inet rules accept TCP/UDP protocol, local/remote addresses, and local/remote
ports.
Ethernet rules accept EAP/WAI ether type and remote MAC address.
All rules require a queue-set ID and may name an interface.
.It Cm remove
.Em State-changing .
Remove the rule identified by UUID.
.El
.Pp
The tested
.Cm show
returned no rules.
The add/remove forms were inspected through usage validation only and were not
executed.
.Ss redirect
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl redirect create -t {ethernet|cellular} [-d delegate] interface
skywalkctl redirect set -d {delegate|none} interface
skywalkctl redirect destroy interface
.Ed
.Pp
Every functional redirect subcommand is
.Em state-changing .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm create
Create a redirect interface of Ethernet or cellular type, optionally with a
delegate interface.
.It Cm set
Change or clear the delegate of an existing redirect interface.
.It Cm destroy
Destroy the named redirect interface.
.El
.Pp
No functional redirect form was intentionally executed.
Notably,
.Dl skywalkctl redirect destroy -h
does not request help on the tested build: it treats
.Dq -h
as an interface name and attempts a destroy operation.
Do not use that form for discovery.
.Ss aop
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl aop [-h] [-b]
.Ed
.Pp
Displays AOP networking or driver statistics.
.Fl b
requests activity bitmaps.
Support is hardware and build dependent.
The tested default form reported
.Dq Operation not supported,
and the bitmap form returned no records.
.Ss print-banner
.Bd -literal -offset indent
skywalkctl print-banner [-h]
.Ed
.Pp
An undocumented/internal banner path.
It returned no output on the tested build.
.Ss channel-stats
Compatibility alias for
.Cm channel .
It accepted the same command and wait filters and produced identical output in
the tested snapshot.
.Ss list-providers
Compatibility alias for
.Cm provider .
It accepted
.Fl D
and produced identical detailed output in the tested snapshot.
.Sh OUTPUT INTERPRETATION
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Empty successful result
Exit 0 with empty standard output and standard error means the command returned
no record at that instant.
.It Permission error
.Dq Operation not permitted
means the requested kernel surface was not fully accessible.
.It Unsupported surface
.Dq Operation not supported
means the hardware or OS build did not expose that data surface.
.It Missing sysctl
.Dq No such file or directory
for a sysctl can indicate that the installed utility and running kernel no
longer agree on that legacy key.
.It Exit 2 from netns
A syntactically valid address/protocol pair did not produce a namespace record
on the tested build.
.It Exit 22
Invalid or incomplete
.Cm netns
arguments.
.It Exit 64 or 255
Usage/argument parser result, not a network finding.
.It Exit 137
An external collector intentionally ended an unbounded wait mode with SIGKILL;
this is not a native
.Nm
diagnostic status.
.El
.Sh PRIVACY
Raw output can fingerprint a host and reveal current network relationships.
Before publication, redact credentials and access-bearing material, plus real
host/user names, IP addresses, ports when identifying, PIDs, runtime UUIDs,
precise timestamps, and application inventory.
Preserve field names, counts, exit statuses, command behavior, and technical
interpretation.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/sbin/skywalkctl
.It Pa /usr/sbin/skywalkctl
Installed utility.
.It Pa /usr/share/man/man8/skywalkctl.8
Apple's installed brief manual page.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
uses command-specific exit statuses.
Observed values include 0 for success, 2 for a valid but unmatched
.Cm netns
selection, 22 for invalid
.Cm netns
arguments, 64 for usage errors, 71 for the stale
.Cm status
sysctl path, and 255 for some top-level subcommand validation failures.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr netstat 1 ,
.Xr lsof 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "Skywalkctl guide contributors"
.Pp
This expanded observational manual was derived from the installed manual,
built-in usage text, an Apple-signed macOS 26.4 binary, elevated read-only
experiments, and Apple's open-source XNU syscall surface.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
is a debug interface with inconsistent help handling and build-dependent
schemas.
Do not infer compromise from an unfamiliar provider, tunnel, channel flag,
empty table, or cumulative counter without independent corroboration.
